2.2.2 Topic Tree
Inspired by (Chen et al.), to address the limited coverage and insufficient richness of manually constructed labels of training data, as shown in Fig. 3, we construct a topic tree through an expert-defined taxonomy and model-based label expansion. Based on the dual dimensions, we construct corresponding two-level labels and ensure their reliability through human curation, forming a foundational knowledge and ability label system. Building upon the two-level labels, we leverage MLLMs to construct topics that combine domain knowledge and abilities, aiming to comprehensively cover the specific applications of MLLMs’ abilities in vertical domains. Ultimately, we arrive at a tree structure for both domain and ability dimensions: first-level (L1) ∼ second-level (L2) labels and third-level topic (L3). The process specifically consists of the following three steps:
(1) Expert definition. Experts manually define the L1 label for both domain and ability dimensions to ensure reliability and coverage.
(2) Label expansion. We carefully design prompts and leverage GPT-4o (Hurst et al., 2024) to expand L2labels based on manually defined L1 labels, aiming to maximize coverage while maintaining decoupled label definitions.
(3) Topic generation. Based on L1 and L2 labels, we use GPT-4o to generate more fine-grained L3 topics, ensuring that each topic reflects the concrete application domains of specific general capabilities, e.g., Counting Problem ∼ Symbol/Sign Counting ∼ Traffic Signal Counting.
Leveraging the semantic comprehension and generative capabilities of MLLMs, we obtain 38,455
