thereby strengthening both multimodal and linguistic capabilities.
• Instruction-preference data. For different vertical domain scenarios, we include preferencetailored data to shape distinct response format styles (e.g., structured markdown and human-like styles).
• Long context data. By increasing the training token limit so the model can ingest and produce longer sequences, we enhance logical reasoning and lay the foundation for subsequent reinforcement learning.
• High-difficulty supplementary data. We employ rejection sampling to extract data that the model completely fails to handle from the reinforcement learning phase (described in Sec. 4), allowing the model to learn it in advance through SFT to lay the groundwork for subsequent reinforcement learning.
4 Hybrid Reinforcement Learning
After the SFT stage, the model acquires initial domain knowledge and achieves a balanced level of multimodal capability. Considering the demonstrated effectiveness and efficiency of the recent post-training paradigm based on RLVR (Reinforcement Learning with Verifiable Rewards) Cao et al. (2024); Zhang et al. (2025a); Guo et al. (2025a); Shao et al. (2024); Hong et al. (2025); Guo et al. (2025b), we further employ RLVR to enhance the model’s reasoning abilities and generalization performance on complex multimodal tasks. However, the conventional RLVR training paradigm faces several bottlenecks when applied to multimodal tasks:
• Exploration-exploitation imbalance and reduced generalization. Existing RLVR reward designs typically optimize the Pass@1 objective. While this raises average performance on standard benchmarks, it biases the model toward imitating previously high-reward behaviors and avoiding low-reward actions. Such imitation narrows the model’s effective inference ability frontier (Yue et al., 2025b; Chen et al., 2025): training does not introduce genuinely new reasoning capabilities and instead suppresses exploratory behavior, leading to the degraded overall generalization.
• Accuracy-diversity trade-off and cognitive rigidification. Verifiable and correctness-focused rewards tend to prioritize accuracy at the expense of diversity (Li et al., 2025a; Song et al., 2025). To maximize measured correctness, training progressively sharpens the output distribution, causing the model’s outputs to converge and the information content to drop substantially. This diversity collapse severely limits the utility of MLLMs in exploratory applications (e.g., creative writing, scientific discovery and open-ended problem solving), and reduces the model’s ability to generalize on domain-specific tasks.
• Advantage-signal collapse during Reinforcement Learning (RL) training. When withingroup response rewards become uniform (all-correct or all-incorrect), the advantage signal vanishes and gradient information becomes ineffective (Yu et al., 2025; Hong et al., 2025). This reduces the proportion of informative queries, lowers training efficiency, and impedes the model’s ability to explore deep, multi-step reasoning.
To alleviate the above issues, we propose a multimodal reinforcement-learning paradigm whose core components are:
• Targeted data collection and cleaning combined with rejection sampling, yielding a highquality RL dataset that supplies both general and domain knowledge, and alleviates training
11


inefficiency caused by advantage collapse.
• A two-stage online-offline hybrid RL scheme that balances text-only and multimodal competencies, progressively improving both general-purpose and domain-specific metrics while mitigating visual neglect.
• A multi-dimensional hybrid reward design that simultaneously promotes improvements in model’s overall performance, encourages exploratory diversity and concise replies.
