Additionally, we also incorporate several techniques to enhance stability and performance during RL training. Following prior works, we remove the KL-penalty term during preference optimization Yu et al. (2025); Shen et al. (2025); Hong et al. (2025), i.e., the reference model imposes no constraint on the actor model. This design fully unleashes the model’s exploratory and reasoning capacity while also improving training throughput. Inspired by DAPO Yu et al. (2025), we further adopt an asymmetric clipping strategy to stabilize optimization while expanding the exploration space for low-probability tokens. Specifically, we apply the clip-higher variant, which decouples the upper and lower clipping bounds, enabling richer policy diversity without compromising optimization stability. To prevent degradation in visual understanding, we freeze the parameters of the visual encoder throughout both two RL stages.
